# KTP retired-credential carrier purge — backup-shaped files older than 90 days.
# Script: /usr/local/bin/ktp-credential-carrier-purge.sh (canonical: KTPInfrastructure/scripts/).
#
# Rotated secrets survive in *.bak-* / *.cfg.bak* / archive copies long after the
# value is dead. A dated rule ages them out unattended; the alternative is another
# census, and that census gave a different number every time it was re-derived.
#
# Matches on NAME and AGE only — it never greps for a secret value.
#
# 05:20 ET Sundays — weekly is enough for a 90-day horizon, and it lands clear of
# the nightly backup and of the Monday 05:00 fleet audit.
#
# STAGED DISABLED: uncomment the job line to activate.
# ⚠️ Run DRY_RUN=1 first and read the output — the first real run deletes
#    everything back to the 90-day horizon in one pass. The script defaults to
#    DRY_RUN=1, so the job line below sets DRY_RUN=0 explicitly.
# ⛔ /opt/backups is excluded and guarded: it holds the only database copies that
#    exist, on their own 28-day prune. The script refuses to run if it is absent.
# ⛔ Shell/client histories are never touched here — they need a deliberate
#    decision, not a name-and-age rule.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
#20 5 * * 0 root DRY_RUN=0 /usr/local/bin/ktp-credential-carrier-purge.sh >> /var/log/ktp-credential-carrier-purge.log 2>&1
